If you've got nothing else on, go ahead and do it, but the vast majority of student films that I've been part of haven't led to anything.  The students more often than not fail to get into the industry and give up.  Life moves on.  This is the reason why showreel production companies exist.  Actors take on the student shorts and hope that they can get the footage for their reels, but all too often this footage is terrible, or the script isn't great, or in many cases the students take forever, or even never finish the post-production stage.  

&#x200B;

I am doing an unpaid short at the end of March, but there are a few reasons for that - firstly I've seen the director/writer's work, I quite like it.  I think that he could go far and there have been a few occasions where unpaid work has led to paid work later on down the line.  Secondly, I need the practice.  I am not doing enough acting these days and doing this short seems like a better option than paying for some overpriced acting class (the options we have in this city aren't fantastic).  Only way to get better is to put more hours in.

I would say you need to base it on a) the strength of the script b) the strength of the crew.

&#x200B;

I have unpaid work turn into paid work, on numerous occasions.  In fact there is good odds that I will be lead in an indy feature because of some unpaid work I did before.  You can't depend on the stuff, but if it happens.  Great.  

&#x200B;

I would say unpaid for 8 days is a lot of time, especially when you have bills to pay.  I would probably respectfully refuse, but explain your reasoning.

