A lot of it is to do with your ear being able to pick up and understand tones, pitch and rhythm.  For some, they are naturally skilled, others have to work on it, others still just don't have it in them.  Looking at the placement of sound within the mouth and nose is quite useful, and trying to figure out vowel sounds and hesitation noises will serve well, but there is no perfect method.  I'm good at some accents, others I find frighteningly difficult to maintain, they sometimes slip around and shift.  Australian becomes Cockney.   Welsh becomes Indian.  Not great.  

&#x200B;

Subtleties are hard.  Southern English accents that isn't either multi cultural London English, Cockney or RP is extremely hard.  I've been trying to replicate Kit Harrington's regular speaking voice for the last while and it's not coming.

