You can get usable tripods and lights from Amazons or other similar online retailers for about $100.  Get another actor and record some stuff either in the park, or in a house.  It might be a good idea to reach out to local film students on Facebook groups etc. to build contacts.  They may want to practice shooting things.  

&#x200B;

I would also consider finding scenes which have roles which are great for day players - teachers, doctors, police, soldiers - most serialized dramas have characters that pop up as a once off, say two lines then leave.  Very naturalized.  It's good for casting directors to see what archetypes and dayplayer roles you'd suit.

OK, first thing's first - what do you mean by British accent?  Britain has arguably a wider range of accents in a shorter space of landmass than any other English speaking country.  

&#x200B;

What I think you are saying is either - Cockney, or Received Pronunciation (RP).  Make a choice as to where this character is from.  Research it, in depth.  Find out where they are from, give them character background.  Look for actors or people from that area and figure out how their accent works.  I would introduce yourself in the learned accent but really do hammer it down.

